  shape.

  [1mData flow.[0m Submit → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m → [38;5;153mlogin()[39m → returns [38;5;153muserId[39m → [38;5;153mSession.save({ userId, username })[39m → [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m with
  the id. Unchanged for the validation-failure branch except as noted below.

  [1mError handling.[0m Three cases, and the first is the one that matters:

  - [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m can throw — Safari private mode, storage disabled, quota. [38;5;153msave[39m wraps in try/catch, warns, and returns
    false. [1mA storage failure must not fail the login.[0m Persistence is a side benefit here, not a precondition.
  - [38;5;153mread()[39m catches JSON parse errors on a corrupted value, clears the key, and returns [38;5;153mnull[39m.
  - A failed or invalid login calls [38;5;153mSession.clear()[39m. Without this, a stale userId from an earlier login in the same tab
    outlives it and reads as the current user — the one genuine bug this feature can introduce.

  [1mSecurity constraint, recorded in the spec.[0m This value is non-authoritative. It's XSS-readable by any script on the
  origin and trivially editable from devtools, so it may be displayed and logged but must never be what the app trusts
  to decide who someone is or what they may do.

  Before I write this up: the repo has no tooling configured at all — no linter, no formatter, no test runner, and
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153mscripts[39m or [38;5;153mdevDependencies[39m. Setting that up is cheapest now, and [38;5;153msession.js[39m is genuinely
  unit-testable (three functions, one dependency you can fake).

